# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must stay LF even in a Windows working tree. A script checked
# out with CRLF and then run inside a Linux container fails with an error that
# has nothing to do with its contents.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Keep the working tree LF on Windows too. Without this, a fresh clone with
# core.autocrlf=true arrives CRLF and the mixed-line-ending hook rewrites
# every file on the first commit, so the first hook run always looks broken.
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Added 2026-08-21. Without these a fresh clone on Windows arrives CRLF, the
# mixed-line-ending hook rewrites the file on the first commit, and every
# frontend commit prints a wall of warnings that trains people to ignore them.
# This subproject is also the first where a file with the wrong line endings
# ends up inside a Linux container.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf

# infra/.env.example is copied to a file compose reads on a Linux host. With
# CRLF every value arrives with a trailing carriage return, so POSTGRES_HOST
# becomes "db\r" and the connection fails for a reason the error does not name.
# It matches none of the rules above, because its extension is `.example`.
*.example text eol=lf

# Added 2026-08-21 by the barrier, before the lanes that create these files
# started. Every one of them ends up inside or under a Linux system, and each
# fails in a way that names something other than line endings: a systemd unit
# with CRLF does not parse, a Dockerfile RUN line with a trailing carriage
# return fails inside the container, and an env file read by compose turns
# POSTGRES_HOST into "db" plus a carriage return, so the failure reads as DNS.
#
# The foundation lane found the .example gap in a file it was creating and
# recommended these four rather than pre-empting three lanes mid-flight. That
# was the right call for an agent and the wrong moment to wait: covering them
# now means the files are covered from birth instead of after a fresh clone on
# Windows has already broken them once.
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf

# Added 2026-08-21 by the barrier, with the file itself. .dockerignore has no
# extension, so none of the rules above reach it, and `* text=auto` leaves the
# working tree to core.autocrlf. A carriage return at the end of a pattern is
# part of the pattern: `data\r` matches nothing, so the directory it was
# written to keep out of a public image would be in it.
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# The last of them. Every one of these showed as modified in `git status` with
# a zero-line diff, because `* text=auto` leaves the working-tree ending to
# core.autocrlf while every rule above pins one. Eighty-six files that say
# "changed" and are not is how a person learns to stop reading git status, and
# this repository leans on people reading output.
#
# .svg is text and Next ships five of them; .mjs is a config file; the three
# dotfiles have no extension for a pattern to catch.
*.svg text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.nvmrc text eol=lf
.prettierignore text eol=lf


# Added 2026-08-21. These four matched none of the rules above and fell through
# to `* text=auto`, which does not decide anything: with core.autocrlf=true it
# lets git guess per file whether the working tree gets LF or CRLF. Every rule
# above this one was added after that guess went wrong somewhere, and two of
# these are read by uv on a Linux runner, which is the same shape.
#
# Measured on 2026-08-21 with `git ls-files --eol`: every tracked file is `i/lf`
# today, so none of this repairs an existing defect. It removes the guessing.
# Stated plainly because an earlier draft of this comment claimed uv.lock was
# committed with carriage returns; that came from counting them in `git show`
# output, which git converts on Windows before printing it, and it was wrong.
#
# The icon is binary. `* text=auto` classifies it correctly today because it
# holds NUL bytes, so this line changes nothing now and stops a future `text`
# rule from catching it and corrupting it.
uv.lock text eol=lf
.python-version text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.ico binary
